// @check
// @error-at 21:12 `Plain` is not a `flat` type, so it has no byte layout
// @error-at 22:10 `flat type Rec` has no field 'nope'
// Reading a layout that does not exist (webcraft P1.2).
//
// `Plain.size` on a NON-flat record is a targeted message naming `flat`, not the
// `undeclared identifier 'Plain'` master answers here. That matters more than it
// looks: `N.f` where `N` is a type name is otherwise a dead end in the language,
// so this message is the whole discoverability path for the feature — someone who
// reaches for `.size` on the wrong kind of record gets told which kind has one.
//
// Answering at all is also a deliberate NON-generalization. A non-flat record's
// layout is WasmGC's business and the compiler does not know it, so folding a size
// for one would be a lie; the reject is the honest answer, not a missing feature.
//
// `Rec.nope` names the two things a flat type's layout does have: `.size`, and one
// offset per DECLARED field. There is no `.offset`/`.width` sub-level — a nested
// record composes by addition instead (`Outer.k + Inner.a`), which is why one
// level of member access is enough (`flat-records-design.md` §5).
type Plain = { p: i32 }
flat type Rec = { a: i32, b: i64 }
print(Plain.size)
print(Rec.nope)
